                              ORDER ON POST-HEARING BRIEFING

         THIS MATTER comes before Patrick Schaefer and Keven Gedko, hearing examiners for

the New Mexico Public Regulation Commission (“Commission”), upon their own motion. At the

close of the evidentiary hearing in this matter, the Hearing Examiners advised the parties of the

potential need for post-hearing briefs and that an Order on Post-Hearing Briefing would follow.

The transcripts of the evidentiary hearing were all filed by July 23, 2026.


IT IS THEREFORE ORDERED:

         A.       Southwestern Public Service Company (“SPS’), Utility Division Staff (“Staff”),

and any other party may provide post-hearing briefing that addresses, at their discretion, any matter

raised at the evidentiary hearing.

         B.       SPS and Staff shall, and any other party may, provide a recommended methodology

to ensure that Production Tax Credits (“PTCs”) accrued in a Deferred Tax Asset by SPS do not

carry forward for an unreasonable amount of time.1




1
 As part of the response, please clarify how the methodology takes into account the following statement made by SPS
in its response to the Hearing Examiners’ Third Bench Request issued during the hearing, in which SPS states: “Please
note that SPS removed the Deferred Tax Asset (“DTA”) associated with all wind PTCs from the Future Test Year
(“FTY”) in accordance with the Final Order in Case No. 17-00044-UT. SPS also did not include in the FTY a return
on the DTA associated with those wind PTCs. Accordingly, the base-rate revenue requirement in this case includes
neither the projected wind PTC benefits nor a return on the associated DTA balance.”
             BEFORE THE NEW MEXICO PUBLIC REGULATION COMMISSION
Order on Post-Hearing Briefing                        Docket No. 25-00079-UT

          C.      SPS and Staff shall, and other parties may, explain whether, and why or why not,

the “Energy-at-Source” allocation of the PTCs provides a fair allocation of the PTCs to the

different rate classes on SPS’s system. The briefing shall take into account the different price per

kWh paid by different rate classes and explain how an allocation of PTCs through the “Energy-at-

Source” allocator is fair to a rate class that pays a higher rate per kWh than another rate class.2

          D.      Post-Hearing Briefs shall be filed by August 14, 2026, and shall not exceed 50

pages in length. Response Briefs shall be filed August 31, 2026, and shall not exceed 25 pages in

length.

          ISSUED under the Seal of the Commission at Santa Fe, New Mexico, on July 28, 2026.


                           NEW MEXICO PUBLIC REGULATION COMMISSION




                                       ___________________________
                                       Patrick Schaefer
